\description{
Singly diagonally implicit Runge-Kutta methods of OrdinaryDiffEqSDIRK for
stiff problems, mass matrix DAEs of index 1 and split problems. Each
implicit stage is solved by the modified Newton iteration of
\code{\link[=ImplicitEuler]{ImplicitEuler()}}, which reuses the Jacobian across stages and steps; the
Jacobian is \code{prob$f$jac} when the problem has one and finite differences
otherwise.
}
\details{
Adaptive methods with an embedded error estimate:
\itemize{
\item \code{TRBDF2()}: the second order A, B, L and S stable TR-BDF2 method, with
a smoothed error estimate; good for loose tolerances.
\item \code{SDIRK2()}: a second order A, B and L stable SDIRK method.
\item \code{Kvaerno3()}, \code{Kvaerno4()}, \code{Kvaerno5()}: A and L stable, stiffly
accurate ESDIRK methods of orders 3, 4 and 5.
\item \code{KenCarp3()}, \code{KenCarp4()}, \code{KenCarp47()}, \code{KenCarp5()},
\code{KenCarp58()}: A and L stable, stiffly accurate ESDIRK methods of orders
3 to 5 (\code{KenCarp47()} with seven, \code{KenCarp58()} with eight stages) that
are additive (IMEX) methods on a \code{\link[=SplitODEProblem]{SplitODEProblem()}}.
\item \code{Cash4()}, \code{Hairer4()}, \code{Hairer42()}: A and L stable fourth order SDIRK
methods. \code{Cash4(embedding = 3)} uses the third order embedded method of
Cash, any other value the second embedding.
\item \code{ESDIRK325L2SA()}, \code{ESDIRK436L2SA2()}, \code{ESDIRK437L2SA()},
\code{ESDIRK547L2SA2()}, \code{ESDIRK54I8L2SA()}, \code{ESDIRK659L2SA()}: the optimized
ESDIRK methods of Kennedy and Carpenter, stiffly accurate with an L
stable embedding, of orders 3 to 6 (the first digit).
}

Fixed step methods (give \code{dt}):
\itemize{
\item \code{ImplicitMidpoint()}: the second order symmetric and symplectic
implicit midpoint rule.
\item \code{SSPSDIRK2()}: a second order strong stability preserving SDIRK method
(SSP coefficient 4).
\item \code{SFSDIRK4()} to \code{SFSDIRK8()}: fourth order SDIRK methods with four to
eight stages.
\item \code{CFNLIRK3()}: a third order IMEX method.
\item \code{ARS222()}, \code{ARS232()}, \code{ARS343()}, \code{ARS443()}: the IMEX methods of
Ascher, Ruuth and Spiteri (ARS(s, sigma, p)).
\item \code{IMEXSSP222()}, \code{IMEXSSP2322()}, \code{IMEXSSP3332()}, \code{IMEXSSP3433()}: the
IMEX strong stability preserving methods of Pareschi and Russo, with an
implicit first stage.
\item \code{BHR553()}: the five stage third order IMEX method BHR(5,5,3)* of
Boscarino and Russo.
}

\code{SDIRK22()} is the \code{\link[=Trapezoid]{Trapezoid()}} method, as in OrdinaryDiffEqSDIRK, where
the constructor returns a \code{Trapezoid} (whose step does not use the stage
predictor).

The IMEX methods (\verb{KenCarp*}, \verb{ARS*}, \verb{IMEXSSP*}, \code{BHR553()} and
\code{CFNLIRK3()}) treat \code{f1} of a \code{\link[=SplitODEProblem]{SplitODEProblem()}} implicitly, with the
Jacobian given to the split function, and \code{f2} explicitly; on other
problems they integrate \code{f} implicitly. The methods with an explicit
first stage (the ESDIRK, \code{TRBDF2()}, \verb{ARS*}, \code{BHR553()} and \code{CFNLIRK3()})
accept only diagonal mass matrices. Dense output is the cubic Hermite
interpolant.
}
\examples{
prob <- ODEProblem(function(u, p, t) -50 * (u - cos(t)), 0, c(0, 1))
sol <- solve(prob, KenCarp4())
sol(1)
solve(prob, TRBDF2(), reltol = 1e-6)$stats$naccept
solve(prob, ImplicitMidpoint(), dt = 0.05)$u[[21]]
sp <- SplitODEProblem(function(u, p, t) -50 * u, function(u, p, t) 50 * cos(t), 0, c(0, 1))
solve(sp, ARS343(), dt = 0.05)$u[[21]]
}
